<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>AWS Secrets Manager and KMS Completion Report</title>
  <style>
    body {
      font-family: Calibri, Arial, sans-serif;
      font-size: 11pt;
      line-height: 1.45;
      color: #1f2933;
      margin: 48px;
    }
    h1 {
      font-size: 22pt;
      margin-bottom: 4px;
      color: #102a43;
    }
    h2 {
      font-size: 15pt;
      margin-top: 24px;
      margin-bottom: 8px;
      color: #243b53;
      border-bottom: 1px solid #d9e2ec;
      padding-bottom: 4px;
    }
    .meta {
      color: #52606d;
      margin: 0;
    }
    .status {
      font-weight: bold;
      color: #0b6b3a;
    }
    code, pre {
      font-family: Consolas, "Courier New", monospace;
      font-size: 10pt;
    }
    pre {
      background: #f5f7fa;
      border: 1px solid #d9e2ec;
      padding: 10px;
      white-space: pre-wrap;
    }
    table {
      border-collapse: collapse;
      width: 100%;
      margin-top: 8px;
    }
    th, td {
      border: 1px solid #d9e2ec;
      padding: 8px;
      text-align: left;
      vertical-align: top;
    }
    th {
      background: #f0f4f8;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <h1>AWS Secrets Manager and KMS Completion Report</h1>
  <p class="meta">Fitness Tracker Project</p>
  <p class="meta">Date: June 6, 2026</p>
  <p class="status">Status: Done</p>

  <h2>Overview</h2>
  <p>
    The Fitness Tracker repository has been wired to use AWS Secrets Manager,
    AWS KMS, and External Secrets Operator for runtime secret management in EKS.
    Sensitive runtime values are no longer expected to be committed as static
    Kubernetes Secret manifests.
  </p>

  <h2>Completed Work</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Created documentation for AWS Secrets Manager and KMS usage in <code>docs/aws-secrets-kms.md</code>.</li>
    <li>Added <code>scripts/aws-secrets-kms-bootstrap.sh</code> to create or update the runtime secret in AWS Secrets Manager.</li>
    <li>Configured the bootstrap script to use a customer-managed AWS KMS key alias: <code>alias/fitness-tracker-secrets</code>.</li>
    <li>Enabled KMS key rotation when the helper script creates the customer-managed key.</li>
    <li>Stored runtime values as JSON properties: <code>MONGODB_URI</code> and <code>DD_API_KEY</code>.</li>
    <li>Integrated the provisioning step into Jenkins under the <code>Provision Runtime Secrets</code> stage.</li>
    <li>Added Helm values for AWS region, IRSA service account name, External Secrets settings, remote secret name, and target Kubernetes Secret name.</li>
    <li>Added Helm templates for <code>SecretStore</code> and <code>ExternalSecret</code> so the cluster can sync secrets from AWS Secrets Manager.</li>
    <li>Updated the application deployment to read <code>MONGODB_URI</code> and <code>DD_API_KEY</code> from the generated Kubernetes Secret.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Secret and Key Details</h2>
  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Item</th>
      <th>Value</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Default AWS region</td>
      <td><code>us-east-1</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Secrets Manager secret name</td>
      <td><code>fitness-tracker/production/runtime</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>KMS alias</td>
      <td><code>alias/fitness-tracker-secrets</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Generated Kubernetes Secret</td>
      <td><code>fitness-tracker-runtime-secrets</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>External Secrets refresh interval</td>
      <td><code>1h</code></td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h2>Runtime Secret JSON Shape</h2>
  <pre>{
  "MONGODB_URI": "mongodb://mongodb-service:27017/fitness-tracker",
  "DD_API_KEY": "replace-with-real-value"
}</pre>

  <h2>CI/CD Flow</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Jenkins receives <code>MONGODB_URI</code> and <code>DD_API_KEY</code> from Jenkins credentials.</li>
    <li>Jenkins runs <code>scripts/aws-secrets-kms-bootstrap.sh</code> with <code>AWS_REGION</code>, <code>SECRET_NAME</code>, and <code>KMS_ALIAS</code>.</li>
    <li>The script creates the KMS key and alias if missing, enables key rotation, and creates or updates the Secrets Manager secret.</li>
    <li>Jenkins installs External Secrets Operator and deploys the Helm chart to the <code>fitness-ns</code> namespace.</li>
    <li>External Secrets Operator syncs the AWS secret into Kubernetes as <code>fitness-tracker-runtime-secrets</code>.</li>
    <li>The application pod consumes <code>MONGODB_URI</code> and <code>DD_API_KEY</code> using <code>secretKeyRef</code>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>IAM Access Required</h2>
  <p>
    The External Secrets Operator service account should use IRSA and an IAM
    role with least-privilege access to the application secret and KMS key.
    Required actions are:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>secretsmanager:GetSecretValue</code></li>
    <li><code>secretsmanager:DescribeSecret</code></li>
    <li><code>kms:Decrypt</code></li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Files Included</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>docs/aws-secrets-kms.md</code></li>
    <li><code>scripts/aws-secrets-kms-bootstrap.sh</code></li>
    <li><code>Jenkinsfile</code></li>
    <li><code>helm/values.yaml</code></li>
    <li><code>helm/templates/secretstore.yaml</code></li>
    <li><code>helm/templates/externalsecret.yaml</code></li>
    <li><code>helm/templates/app-deployment.yaml</code></li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Deployment Notes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Replace placeholder AWS account IDs and role ARNs with the real production AWS account details before live deployment.</li>
    <li>Keep real <code>MONGODB_URI</code> and <code>DD_API_KEY</code> values in Jenkins/AWS only; do not commit them to source control.</li>
    <li>Do not commit static Kubernetes Secret manifests for these runtime values.</li>
    <li>After deployment, verify ExternalSecret status and confirm the application pod starts with <code>secretKeyRef</code> values resolved.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Verification Commands</h2>
  <pre>kubectl get externalsecret -n fitness-ns
kubectl get secret fitness-tracker-runtime-secrets -n fitness-ns
kubectl rollout status deployment/fitness-tracker-app -n fitness-ns --timeout=300s</pre>

  <h2>Conclusion</h2>
  <p>
    Secret Manager and KMS integration is completed for the Fitness Tracker
    project. Runtime secrets are centralized in AWS Secrets Manager, encrypted
    with a customer-managed KMS key, synced into EKS through External Secrets
    Operator, and consumed by the application deployment without storing
    sensitive values in the repository.
  </p>
</body>
</html>
